<Layout title="Research">
  <h1>Research</h1>
  <p>
    Rotator cuff tears are among the most common shoulder injuries, and
    surgery can reattach the tendon, but the muscle often doesn't come
    back. It wastes away, fills with fat and scar tissue, and those changes
    can persist long after repair. My research asks whether a material
    injected into the muscle itself can change that healing environment.
  </p>
  <p class="pub-meta">
    Jump to: <a href="#current">Current research</a> &middot;
    <a href="#publications">Publications &amp; presentations</a> &middot;
    <a href="#earlier">Earlier research</a>
  </p>


  <div class="card" id="current">
    <h3>MuscleMatrix</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">Scientific Consultant &middot; August 2026 &ndash; Present</p>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;">
      From the Bench Toward the Clinic
    </p>
    <p>
      MuscleMatrix is an early-stage startup built partly on the hydrogel
      technology I co-invented during my PhD, developing an injectable,
      cell-free hyaluronic acid scaffold to support muscle repair after
      rotator cuff injury. As a scientific consultant, I'm helping build the
      company from the ground up, which includes setting up a lab from scratch, regulatory
      and safety compliance, sourcing manufacturers and suppliers, FDA
      regulatory strategy, and grant writing. I also continue formulation
      work on injectability and mechanics, and train new scientific staff on
      the hydrogel platform and complex technical procedures.
    </p>
    <a class="btn secondary" href="https://ipira.berkeley.edu/musclematrix" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      Learn More About MuscleMatrix
    </a>
  </div>

  <div class="card">
    <h3>Kevin Healy Lab, UC Berkeley</h3>
    <p class="pub-meta">PhD Candidate &middot; January 2022 &ndash; August 2026</p>

    <p style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--pink-700); margin-bottom: 0.2rem;">
      Viscoelastic Hyaluronic Acid Hydrogels for Muscle Regeneration
    </p>
    <p>
      I engineer tunable, semi-synthetic hyaluronic acid (HyA) hydrogels
      whose viscoelastic mechanics are designed to support muscle
      regeneration and vascularization after rotator cuff injury and
      volumetric muscle loss. Much of my work is on the
      materials side, where I tune and characterize the hydrogels' mechanics and
      injectability and develop drug-loaded versions carrying formoterol.
    </p>
    <p>
      To understand <em>why</em> the hydrogels work, I study how they direct
      the differentiation of fibro-adipogenic progenitors (FAPs), the
      muscle-resident cells that drive fat buildup after injury, and how that
      supports the fusion of muscle stem cells, using 3D cultures and
      cocultures. I also performed the single-cell RNA-seq analysis of
      patient-derived FAPs and used the findings to guide hydrogel design,
      from degradation rate and ligand presentation to drug release and
      injectability. In collaboration with orthopedic surgeons at UCSF and
      UVA, I prepared hydrogels for and analyzed data from in vivo injury
      studies.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a mouse model of delayed rotator cuff repair, the hydrogel improved
      recovery of muscle quality and vascularization. This work has been
      published in <em>Advanced Healthcare Materials</em>
      and presented at meetings of the Biomedical Engineering Society, the
      Society For Biomaterials, the Orthopaedic Research Society, and
      Biological Scaffolds for Regenerative Medicine.
    </p>
